/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch30_bed_correlations  --  where do Dax, k_f and k_LDF come from?

  batch13's bed (15 % CO2 on 13X beads, 0.5 m, u = 0.05 m/s, 1 bar, 298 K)
  with the carrier changed to N2 (the catalogue's helium has no diffusion
  volume) and the three transport numbers put beside what the literature
  correlations say.  The case DECLARES Dax and k_LDF, as every adsorber case
  does, and a declared value always wins -- so the front below is the
  declared one.  `transportCorrelations` makes the run ALSO print and
  publish (KPIs corr_*) every correlation's estimate, each with its
  validity window and the page its coefficients were read at:

    axial dispersion  Wakao & Funazkri 1978 eq (2); Edwards & Richardson
                      1968 eq (17) (gas); Chung & Wen 1968 eqs (15)-(16)
                      (liquids); Gunn 1987 eq (1)
    film k_f          Wakao & Funazkri 1978 eq (12); Wilson & Geankoplis
                      1966 eqs (8)-(9) (liquids)
    k_LDF (series)    Ruthven 1984 eq (8.41): film + macropore + micropore,
                      the internal terms with Glueckauf's 15 (1955 eqn 3a)

  BASIS.  Dax is stored on the BED (superficial) basis, Dax = eps D_L; the
  correlations are written for the interstitial D_L and are converted (the
  run prints both).  A textbook D_L typed into Dax overstates the
  dispersion by 1/eps.

  WHAT THE RUN SHOWS (feed state: rho = 1.227 kg/m3, mu = 1.72e-5 Pa s,
  Re = 7.13, Sc = 0.854, Dm = 1.64e-5 m2/s from Fuller):
    * Dax (bed basis, m2/s): Edwards & Richardson 3.5e-5 and Gunn 3.2e-5
      agree within 10 % and sit BELOW the declared 1e-4 (batch13's Pe = 250
      is a conservative teaching value, not a prediction); Wakao & Funazkri
      3.8e-4 is HIGHER because its stagnant term 20 Dm (a particle whose
      surface controls the transfer) dominates at this Re; Chung & Wen
      1.75e-4 is flagged OUTSIDE its phase (a liquid correlation) and the
      run says so -- the window is the lesson;
    * the film coefficient is k_f = 0.044 m/s (Wakao & Funazkri, Sh = 5.39;
      Wilson & Geankoplis answers 0.041 but is flagged: liquids, Sc 950 up);
    * the series k_LDF = 0.0113 1/s, against the declared 0.05: the shares
      of 1/k say WHERE the resistance is -- film 4.3 %, macropore 20.0 %,
      micropore 75.7 % -- and the declared value, 4.4 times faster, is
      published beside the estimate, not used (the front below is the
      declared one: t_b(5 %) = 2934.07 s, as batch13).
  The estimates need particle data the catalogue cannot hold (porosity,
  tortuosity, micropore diffusivity of THIS sample), declared below as
  sample data with their scope.

  Tolerances: the golden pins the estimates to 1e-4 relative; the anchors
  of every correlation are checked by check_bed_correlations.py.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        bed;
        type        fixedBedAdsorber;

        operation
        {
            adsorbent   zeolite13X_beads;   // case-local identity (dParticle)
            L           0.5   m;       // bed length
            area        0.01  m2;      // cross-section
            eps         0.4;           // interparticle void fraction
            u           0.05  m/s;     // superficial, CONSTANT DECLARED
            T           298   K;       // isothermal DECLARED
            P           1     bar;     // CONSTANT DECLARED
            Dax  [0 2 -1 0 0]  1e-4;   // m2/s -- BED basis (eps D_L), DECLARED:
                                       // it wins over every estimate below
            nCells      100;           // axial mesh DECLARED

            feed    { molarComposition { CO2 0.15; N2 0.85; } }
            initial { molarComposition { N2  1.0; } }   // clean bed: q = 0

            kLDF                       // EQUIPMENT/SAMPLE data: declared, wins
            {
                basis    solidFilm;    // dq/dt = k*(q* - q)
                scope    "2 mm 13X beads, teaching bed batch30";
                source   "declared spec value (the batch13 anchor); Glueckauf k = 15 De/rp^2 gives the order";
                k
                {
                    CO2  [0 0 -1 0 0]  0.05;   // 1/s
                }
            }

            transportCorrelations
            {
                dispersion { model EdwardsRichardson; species CO2; }
                film       { model WakaoFunazkri; }
                series
                {
                    CO2
                    {
                        scope  "2 mm 13X beads, teaching bed batch30";
                        source "ASSUMED teaching values, typical of binder-bonded zeolite beads (Ruthven 1984 ch. 6-7: macropore porosity 0.3-0.4, tortuosity 2-6); measure them on the real sample before design use";
                        epsP  0.35;            // macropore porosity of the bead
                        tauP  3.0;             // macropore tortuosity, Dp = Dm/tauP
                        micropore { DcOverRc2 1e-3; }   // Dc/rc^2 [1/s], crystal
                    }
                }
                compare true;          // publish EVERY registered model's estimate
            }
        }
    }
);
